        "t1": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Boca Raton from the beach to West Boca</strong></p><p>Boca Raton starts at the A1A condos and the beach parks at Red Reef and Spanish River. It runs across the Intracoastal and Lake Boca, past Mizner Park and Florida Atlantic University, to the country-club communities west of I-95. Royal Palm Yacht and Country Club, The Sanctuary and the Lake Boca lots have seawalls and pool decks on the water. Boca West, Broken Sound, Woodfield and Boca Pointe are gated, and their associations review exterior changes before the city does.</p><p>The housing stock runs from Mizner-era Mediterranean homes in Old Floresta to 1980s ranch houses on golf courses and new builds with open stairs. Pools are common, so a glass pool fence is often the first project an owner plans. Delray Beach sits just north, and Deerfield Beach, the first city in Broward, just south.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t2": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>The rules: Palm Beach County, HOAs and the pool act</strong></p><p>Boca Raton is in Palm Beach County, outside the High-Velocity Hurricane Zone. It is still a high design wind speed area under the Florida Building Code, 8th edition (2023). The City of Boca Raton building division reviews railing attachments on permit applications; West Boca addresses go through Palm Beach County. The residential code sets a 36-inch guard for a house deck or balcony more than 30 inches above grade; condos along A1A need 42 inches.</p><p>The Residential Swimming Pool Safety Act sets the pool barrier at 48 inches on the outside. Gates must close on their own and open away from the pool. Our 46-inch panel reaches that height, and the 57.7-inch panel gives 60 inches when a community wants more. HOA architectural committees usually ask for drawings, finish samples and product data before the permit, so start there. The <a href=\"/pages/florida-building-code-for-glass-railings\">Florida building code page</a> has the section references.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t3": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Delivery to Boca Raton and DIY installation</strong></p><p>Standard orders are processed in 2 to 3 business days and arrive in 5 to 10 business days by freight carrier with a liftgate. In a gated community, give the guardhouse the carrier's name so the truck gets through, and have someone home to sign and inspect. Custom sizes take 2 to 3 weeks in transit after processing. Orders over 3,000 dollars ship free, and the checkout price is final, with no duties, tariffs or hidden fees.</p><p>The system is designed for DIY. Each panel sits in two spigots, with no posts, so a pool fence goes up one panel at a time. Follow the installation guide and videos; most standard jobs take about 4 hours. Spigots fix to the concrete pool deck or to wood, with screws sold separately. Report any shipping damage within 3 days of delivery and before installation for a free replacement or refund.</p>"
          }
        }
